[Music]  on this saturday night the range of  responses to the rapid spread of omicron  from shorter isolation to booster shots  coming sooner shorter incubation period  means that people are infectious earlier  why one province isn't reimposing  restrictions plus scaled back new year's  celebrations in the u.s as covid19 cases  surge our hospitals are roughly the same  numbers they were on the worst day of  last winter and residents return home to  ruins after a ferocious colorado  wildfire forces tens of thousands to  flee over the holidays  [Music]  global national  reporting tonight  good evening and thanks for joining us  canadians are starting off the new year  on a high that no one wanted record  covet 19 case numbers are still being  seen across the country prompting  several provinces to tighten  restrictions and extend the winter break  but there is the growing consensus the  omicron variant causes milder illnesses  with a shorter incubation period while  more canadians are getting infected the  isolation period for some is getting  shorter ross lord has our top story  tonight  with the number of new covet 19 cases  reaching record highs every day even  those leading canada's response are  feeling the impact the latest to be  infected new brunswick premier blaine  higgs despite being fully vaccinated  including a booster shot several days  ago i came down with a cold what seemed  to be cold symptoms in any way  wednesday morning i did the test and it  came back positive confronting an  exploding case count you brunswick is  postponing non-urgent surgeries it's  also reducing isolation periods to five  days joining a growing list of other  provinces including british columbia  what we're seeing with omicron is that  shorter incubation period means that  people are infectious earlier  from one to two days before their  symptoms start and that the illness  tends to resolve more quickly other  movements gaining momentum fast tracking  booster shots and making health care  workers and the most vulnerable the  priority for pcr tests now that the  virus is overwhelmed testing capacity so  use the capacity you have in the  smartest possible manner and focus it on  areas that you need it the most  responses to the omicron wave are all  over the map from quebec imposing a  curfew and closing restaurant dining  rooms to saskatchewan rejecting calls to  introduce new gathering restrictions  i've never seen a situation where we're  in the throes of extreme exponential  growth where we're threatening  restrictions and possibly even lockdowns  and talking to school closures and yet  the restaurant so full the malls are  full you have people who are throwing  parties  dionandin says the best new measure  governments can take against omicron is  universal paid sick leave limiting  transmission by making it easier for  working-class canadians to stay home  british columbia is starting 2022 by  providing five days of paid sick leave  similar protection for workers in  private industries that are federally  regulated still has no start date ross  lord global news  and as ross mentioned saskatchewan is  one of the few provinces choosing not to  impose new restrictions despite record  high case numbers but as cases rise  there is concern they will cause a  strain on hospitals and the burden will  fall once again on health care workers  nathaniel dove reports  new year's day is normally filled with  optimism but one saskatchewan doctor is  very worried about what 2022 will bring  we pretty much know what it's going to  look like there's going to be a ton of  cases there's going to be a ton of  outbreaks  there were no gathering restrictions  over the holidays in saskatchewan  instead the government is preaching  personal responsibility  dr alex wong says that didn't work out  well last year especially when the delta  variant pummeled the province icus were  so overloaded patients had to be  transferred to ontario we're going to be  in the exact same situation that we were  in with alpha and delta where we're  scrambling  where we're having to do all kinds of  horrible things in terms of compromising  care acutely for critically ill people  omicron appears to be milder than delta  but it spreads quickly more cases means  more care which means more strain on a  health care system that's still  recovering from a devastating fourth  wave saskatchewan ended 2021 with a new  record high daily case count but premier  scott moe says the focus for now is on  hospital and icu admissions an  epidemiologist believes those numbers  will soon start increasing it will start  to go up and when it starts to go up to  do things then is a little bit too late  all the outbreaks that are going to  occur in schools  all these other pieces are you know  extremely damaging to society not the  start to the new year anyone was hoping  for nathaniel dove global news  saskatoon the u.s is ringing in the new  year with doctors warning of a blizzard  of new covid 19 cases while many new  year's eve celebrations were scaled back  or canceled millions attended events in  major cities as jennifer johnson reports  medical experts say the country's health  care system is going to pay the price  [Music]  the iconic ball drop in times square  with one-fourth of the usual crowd all  masked and vaccinated the city is facing  a huge uptick in covet 19 cases forcing  everything from broadway to businesses  to some subway routes to shut down the  new mayor promises things will get  better not only am i physically walking  into city hall but hope is walking into  city hall  but there were no restrictions in las  vegas where about 300 000 people cheered  along the strip  or places like idaho where a large  unmasked crowd watched the famous potato  drop with the omicron variant spreading  like wildfire healthcare workers are  bracing for what's coming in early 2022.  more than 2 million new covenant 19  cases were reported this past week today  we broke our single day record for  coveted missions this is really a  frightening scenario  almost every governor in the u.s has had  to call up the national guard to help in  hospitals and testing sites our  hospitals right now are roughly the same  numbers they were on the worst day of  last winter's surge this kaiser  permanente rose parade float tells the  story of just how bad the situation is  it never made it out of the warehouse  after medical workers slated to ride on  it were told to go back to their  hospitals the new year starting on an  ominous note for those on the front  lines  jennifer johnson global news washington  coming up the new year's rush that  sparked a deadly stampede at an indian  shrine  because we shared him with the world  you shared part of the love you held for  him  with us  archbishop desmond tutu's daughter  shared her family's gratitude to  mourners around the world as the south  african anti-apartheid hero and nobel  peace prize winner was laid to rest in  cape town remembered as the country's  national conscience tutu passed away on  sunday at the age of 90.  a new year's day stampede has killed at  least 12 people at one of india's  holiest hindu sites 15 others were also  injured during the crowd surge at the  vashno devi shrine in indian-controlled  kashmir where tens of thousands of  hindus gathered for a pilgrimage initial  reports suggest a scuffle between some  devotees may have led to the crush an  investigation is underway to determine  an official cause  in colorado heavy snow and frigid  temperatures overnight helped extinguish  the most destructive wildfire in the  state's history but the weather is  adding to the despair of hundreds of  residents trying to salvage what remains  of their homes the rare winter wildfire  fueled by a windstorm tore through the  suburbs north of denver on thursday two  people are missing and more than 500  homes destroyed  mary simon in her own words ahead what's  on the agenda for canada's first  indigenous governor general  in a year where canadians confronted the  painful reality of the country's  treatment of indigenous peoples there  was also a historic first mary simon was  appointed as governor general the first  indigenous person to become the queen's  representative in canada in a special  interview airing tomorrow on the west  block her excellency mary simon says she  is used to living in two worlds and it's  her goal to bring canadians together  mercedes stevenson brings us more from  her conversation with the governor  general  governor general mary simon understands  the north it is after all where she grew  up there she learned important lessons  especially from her grandmother lessons  that guide her now in her role as  canada's governor general she would say  a word  it's  a yuinata  and it means uh commitment  key let's keep going  don't give up  and that was always a word that we used  in our family but it's also it's all so  used every day in my culture in the  inuit culture  simon is making history as the first  indigenous governor general and she  wants to be a unifying force for canada  an embodiment for reconciliation a  journey that she says must be  accelerated and engaged in by all  canadians a critically important step  forward according to simon is fixing the  inequalities in education faced by  indigenous youth especially in the north  down here you have  the opportunity to stay home or to go  away the up north or in remote areas you  don't have that choice if you want to go  to post-secondary education you have to  move away  and indigenous people are very  close-knit  family  and it's difficult for people to move  away and especially when  young people  have families when they are quite young  someday her excellency mary simon would  like to see a northern university and  indigenous post-secondary institutions  simon believes the discovery of unmarked  residential graves made the wrongs of  the past much more real in the minds of  canadians this year but she says  reconciliation isn't just about what  governments can do to address the issue  there's also the relationship building  that has to occur between people  individuals and other  you know cultures and other societies  have to  build a better relationship a better  understanding have more respect for each  other  simon was also blunt about her view of  the mental health care system in canada  its importance and its lack of resources  two things she hopes to shine a light on  saying it is critically important under  the strain of the covid19 pandemic and  key to addressing the tragedy of the  high suicide rate in northern  communities so as a country we have put  a lot of emphasis on physical health  and we're able to you know get the kind  of treatment we we need in terms of our  physical well-being but in terms of our  mental health we it's very much  on a much lower standard of health care  and we need to address that you can  catch our full conversation with  governor general mary simon coming up on  the west block mercedes stephenson  global news ottawa

